<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    The keyboard picture: the board it is drawn on, the key cap's bridge, and the legend roles the
    cap's own content carries.

    The cap's chrome — the face, the border, the four states and their precedence — is no longer
    here at all: it is the `KeyCapButton` control theme in Themes/ControlThemes/Keycap.axaml, which
    owns the template outright. This file only points the class at it. The layer switch has left
    too: it is a ListBox on the `SegmentedControl` theme, so the view names the theme and there is
    no pill class left to style.
  -->

  <!-- The board the picture sits on: an inset surface, so the caps read as raised out of it. -->
  <Style Selector="Border.keyboardBoard">
    <Setter Property="Background" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceInsetBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderBrush" Value="{DynamicResource SurfaceLineBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="BorderThickness" Value="1" />
    <Setter Property="CornerRadius" Value="{DynamicResource RadiusPanel}" />
    <Setter Property="Padding" Value="{DynamicResource PaddingInspectorSection}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    One key cap. Nothing but the bridge: `KeyCapButton` carries the geometry, the 9/400 legend step,
    the face ladder, the four state badges and the concentric selection/focus rings.

    A class style here would outrank every one of those, so this style deliberately sets no other
    property — `Padding` most of all, which the theme fixes at 2 and to which the hatch overlay's
    `Margin="-2"`, the remap bar's `Margin="-2"` and the selection ring's `Margin="-1"` are all
    tuned.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Button.keyCap">
    <Setter Property="Theme" Value="{StaticResource KeyCapButton}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    The legend roles the cap's own content carries: the caption, the caption's stacked variant, and
    the silkscreen sub-legend. Only what differs from what the cap already inherits — the theme sets
    9/400 on the Button itself, so an ordinary caption needs no type of its own and this style is
    colour alone. The other two step the size down, and each says in `Themes/Typography.axaml` which
    number it takes and why.

    There is deliberately no `.keyCapText.modified` rule any more. A remapped cap used to lift its
    caption to the accent on top of a 14% accent face and an accent border; mockups 1e/2a draw it as
    an ordinary raised cap wearing only the 2px bar, and three accent signals for one state on a
    26px cap is noise. The bar is the remap signal — see Themes/ControlThemes/Keycap.axaml.
  -->
  <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).keyCapText">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}" />

    <!--
      NO `LineHeight`, HERE OR ON THE SUB-LEGEND BELOW, AND THAT IS THE POINT.

      Both used to set one — 9 on a 9px caption, 7 on a 7px legend — justified as "line-height 1,
      the design's own rule for single-line UI text, and how the mockups set every cap
      (`font: 400 9px/1`)". THAT REASONING TRANSFERS A CSS IDIOM TO A DIFFERENT ENGINE and it cost
      the whole board: CSS `line-height: 1` only removes leading and lets the glyphs spill out of
      the line box, while Avalonia's `LineHeight` SIZES the line box and draws nothing past it.

      Concretely, and worth keeping because it is not guessable: Avalonia pins the baseline at the
      font's own ascent below the top of each line and truncates the block at `nLines x LineHeight`.
      IBM Plex Sans is 1.3em tall (ascent 1.025em, descent 0.275em, no line gap), so at 9px the
      baseline alone lands at 9.225 — a 9px line box cuts ABOVE it. Every cap on the board lost
      ~2.7px off its bottom: `Esc` read `Fsc`, `Space` read `Snace`, `Q` read `O`, and the second
      line of `Caps\nLock` went altogether. Themes/Typography.axaml already states this rule for the
      whole app ("a literal LineHeight == FontSize would cut the descenders off every label"); these
      two styles were the only place breaking it.

      The room the old values were buying is bought properly instead, in Controls/KeyCapView.axaml:
      the content grid reclaims the cap's 2px padding band (`Margin="0,-2"`, which moves no badge),
      the sub-legend is not drawn on a lighting board at all, and a caption carrying its own line
      break steps down to `FontSizeKeycapLabelStacked`. See that file for the vertical budget.
    -->
  </Style>
  <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).keyCapText.locked">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextMutedBrush}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    A caption that carries a line break of its own — `Caps\nLock`, `Page\nDown`
    (specs/05-key-model.md §1.1). Two 9px lines measure 23.4px against a 24px content box, which
    leaves nothing for the LED strip a lighting board draws under them, so the stacked case takes
    the step that fits: `FontSizeKeycapLabelStacked` (8), declared beside the caption step with its
    reasoning. Only the size moves — the family, the weight and the colour are the caption's own.
  -->
  <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).keyCapText.stacked">
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeKeycapLabelStacked}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    The silkscreen sub-legend, one step under the caption and one step dimmer, so "printed on the
    cap" and "what this key now does" are never mistaken for each other.

    THE STEP IS OFF THE PUBLISHED SCALE, and knowingly — but it is still a token, because the type
    scale lives in Themes/ and a bare size in a style is the same category of mistake as a hex.
    `FontSizeKeycapSubLegend` (7) is declared beside `FontSizeKeycapLabel` (9) and carries the
    reasoning: mockup 1e sets this legend at 6.5px against a 10px caption, this app 7 against 9, and
    both pairs sit outside the handoff's scale because the scale has no step for board silkscreen.
    The whole board scales up as a unit, so 7 is the authored size rather than the drawn one.
  -->
  <Style Selector=":is(TextBlock).keyCapSubText">
    <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="{DynamicResource TextSecondaryBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="{DynamicResource FontSizeKeycapSubLegend}" />

    <!-- No `LineHeight` here either, and for the reason spelled out on `.keyCapText` above: the old
         7 cut the descender off `play` and `speed`. The natural 9.1px box is what the cap's budget
         is now written against. -->

    <!--
      THE TWO LINE BOXES OVERLAP BY 2px, ON PURPOSE, AND THAT IS WHAT THIS MARGIN SAYS.

      A line box is taller than the ink in it: at the 9px caption step Plex Sans puts the baseline
      9.2px down and reserves 2.5 more for a descent that `F1`, `2` and `;` do not use, and this
      legend's own box opens with 2.3px of leading above its cap height. So the caption's last 2px
      and this legend's first 2px are, on the captions the board actually carries, empty in both.
      Tucking into them costs nothing visible — the caption's baseline still sits 5px clear of this
      legend's cap top — and it buys two things a 26px cap has no other source for:

        - the pair drops from 23px of the cap's 24 to 20, which is the difference between a budget
          that fits and one that fits with room; and
        - THE CAPTION MOVES 2px DOWN, out from under the badges. The advisory bar and the macro dot
          own the band 2-5px inside the cap's border (Themes/ControlThemes/Keycap.axaml), and with
          the caption leading the stack — which is the order mockup 1e draws and the hierarchy the
          design asks for — a cap carrying both a silkscreen and a badge had the mark landing across
          the top of its own legend. At 2px down the caption's cap height starts at 4.9 and the bar
          ends at 5.

      Mockup 1e sets a positive 2px gap here instead, but it draws the pair on a cap 31px tall
      (29x31) while the handoff pins this board's at 30x26. There is no version of that gap that
      fits five pixels less of cap.
    -->
    <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,-2,0,0" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    The recording pulse: the app's only looping motion, and the only one that is not a transition.

    It marks the two surfaces that are waiting for a physical keystroke — the cap that is listening
    for a remap, and the banner over a macro that is recording. One style with a union selector,
    because both pulse the same way and the budget allows exactly one pulse.

    The duration is written out rather than bound: `Themes/Motion.axaml` records it as
    `DurationRecordingPulse`, but an `Animation` is not part of the logical tree, so a
    `DynamicResource` on it would resolve to nothing and silently give a zero-length loop. It also
    needs no reduce-motion variant — a slow opacity cycle is a fade, which reduce-motion keeps.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Button.keyCap.listening, :is(TextBlock).recording">
    <Style.Animations>
      <Animation Duration="0:0:1.4" IterationCount="Infinite" Easing="SineEaseInOut">
        <KeyFrame Cue="0%">
          <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1.0" />
        </KeyFrame>
        <KeyFrame Cue="50%">
          <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="0.55" />
        </KeyFrame>
        <KeyFrame Cue="100%">
          <Setter Property="Opacity" Value="1.0" />
        </KeyFrame>
      </Animation>
    </Style.Animations>
  </Style>

  <!-- The layer switch has no style of its own any more. It is a ListBox on the `SegmentedControl`
       theme (Themes/ControlThemes/Segmented.axaml), which carries the trough, the segments and the
       cleared `Transitions` the switch needs — layer switching is deliberately unanimated, see
       Themes/Motion.axaml. The view names that theme directly, because a ListBox already has a
       theme-shaped API and a class would only add a level of indirection with nothing in it. -->
</Styles>
